From 2c9650411f55877725dc588aa7f33b9f48786ebb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Gehrmann <marius@gehrmann.solutions>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:13:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] drm: apple: Start and connect the T8122 dcpext as its
 firmware expects

Three things the T8122 dcpext firmware (14.7, classic IOMFB template)
needs and the shared paths do differently; each applies to
DCP_FIRMWARE_V_14_7_TEMPLATE only.

PMP: the display gate gives the dcpext a PMP DISPEXT request
(apple,pmp-report) when the PMP runs. As T6030 does for its dcpext
(dcpext_check_power()), run the processor only once the PMP has
acknowledged that request, in the bind before the CPU RUN bit is set
and RTKit is woken. Without it the first modeset hangs. The internal
display has started the PMP long before, so the wait is short;
apple_pmp_report_wait_ready() reads the acknowledgment once after the
PMP start, so poll it (20 ms) for up to 3 s while it returns -EIO. On
any failure, or without a PMP, the processor is started anyway, with
one log line saying so.

EDID: dcpavserv_copy_edid() sends Aurora's T6030 "open" call (DPAV
service group 4, command 6) before the first EDID read. The dcpext
never answers it: copy_edid times out, the firmware drops and retrains
the link, and the connector flaps every ~4 s. AsahiLinux/linux#607
reads the EDID with group 1, command 7 directly; send the open call only
on Aurora's DCPLink session (DCP_FIRMWARE_V_14_7), as before.

DPTX connect: the fabric validates the target (group 0, cmd 12),
connects with the supports-HPD attribute, answers GET_SUPPORTS_HPD with
1, asserts HPD right after request_display and acknowledges
SetTiledDisplayHints with 1. #607 does none of that: it connects with 0,
answers 0, asserts HPD only after the link-configuration wait (that HPD
starts link training) and acknowledges the hints with 0. Do it the #607
way; a link-configuration timeout before the late HPD is expected and
not an error.

Tested on J613 with the rest of the series: rear and front USB-C port,
hotplug, DPMS, 1920x1080@60 and 3440x1440@100.

Link: https://github.com/AsahiLinux/linux/pull/607
